ctions are analytic about $x=2.$ So, $x=2$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-342275/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a9f9cf-06f6-51b8-ba41-a8b6300a5c2b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342275"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-342295/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "88980d21-935e-54c5-99ba-169928576e1e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342295/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "8ab77526-2219-5be0-bcb5-485b389ce9f0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342295/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "cfe7e8e6-0b6f-588c-b4d2-1fa5f36851b7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342295/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "a0aeb3b5-955e-5086-a3a9-ae02e7bed28b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342295/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "198b79f0-7c8e-5800-913f-506c51b61e50" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342295/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a43d43fa-9e98-5969-b1df-7578f6a280fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-342295/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-342295/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-342295/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-342295/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-342295/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-342295/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342295" :question/id #uuid "d6fe8bd2-84b4-5110-99b1-73e60ad5751a" :question/math-academy-id "q-342295" :question/problem "$$\n(x+3)(x-2){y}^{″}+(x-1){y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is an **ordinary** point of the differential equation above?\n\n- $x=-3$\n- $x=0$\n- $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both coefficients $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x+3)(x-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)(x-2){y}^{″}+(x-1){y}^{′}+y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{x-1}{(x+3)(x-2)}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+3)(x-2)}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{x-1}{(x+3)(x-2)}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+3)(x-2)}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check the analyticity of these functions at the given points.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=-3,$ since both denominators at $x=-3$ equal zero. So, $x=-3$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=0.$ So, $x=0$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=1.$ So, $x=1$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-342295/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "12a9f9cf-06f6-51b8-ba41-a8b6300a5c2b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342295"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-342303/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "9a77e7a4-7eae-5244-bea8-5e61a8289a59" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342303/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "39053a45-8497-5718-af86-4ae812fd23d6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342303/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "a097c014-0d21-5ab8-a131-6f0e17a8cea0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342303/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" :answer/id #uuid "4993ddf1-4ba1-5898-974f-c03c48d2fbe7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342303/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "b03742f0-51c4-5956-85a6-e4350a0e5299" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342303/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "0ddee356-ab9a-5884-a0e2-ae99775f1637" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-342303/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer-field/choices ["q-342303/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-342303/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-342303/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-342303/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" "q-342303/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342303" :question/id #uuid "d21da441-5192-5db2-a0e2-e90c0bb5c1ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-342303" :question/problem "$$\n({x}^{2}+1){y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The functions $P(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}$ are analytic about $x=-1.$\n- The functions $(x+1)P(x)$ and $(x+1)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=-1.$\n- The point $x=-1$ is a **regular** singular point of the differential equation." :question/worked-solution "A singular point $x=a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $({x}^{2}+1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}+1){y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=-1.$ So, statement I is true.\n\n- Since $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=-1,$ multiplying them by $(x+1)$ and $(x+1)^{2},$ respectively, they remain analytic about $x=-1.$ Therefore, statement II is true.\n\n- Since statement I is true, $x=-1$ is an ordinary point, not a singular point. Thus, statement III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-342303/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e7d1368-06ee-5135-b9f4-d3d4d2756b32"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342303"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-342479/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "5fca9641-c958-573c-87fd-21bd825bf60a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342479/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" :answer/id #uuid "415b2b17-29b4-5597-8a35-71e9e4c5b60b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342479/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "e0b65824-dcf2-5d86-bf16-a638209b9522" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342479/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "9949e354-b210-56da-b3e0-c18b7250367a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342479/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "2a2260d7-1bd0-5fea-8405-43931a9a42fe" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342479/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "06a01b36-d5db-5e73-88c5-d91847dd22a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-342479/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer-field/choices ["q-342479/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-342479/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" "q-342479/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-342479/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-342479/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342479" :question/id #uuid "38426d90-d9de-5708-ba65-07387f5cbef9" :question/math-academy-id "q-342479" :question/problem "$$\n({x}^{2}-25){y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The functions $P(x)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}-25}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)}{{x}^{2}-25}$ are analytic about $x=5.$\n- The functions $(x-5)P(x)$ and $(x-5)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=5.$\n- The point $x=5$ is a **regular** singular point of the differential equation." :question/worked-solution "A singular point $x=a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $({x}^{2}-25):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-25){y}^{″}+2x{y}^{′}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}-25}{y}^{′}+\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)}{{x}^{2}-25}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}-25}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)}{{x}^{2}-25}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Since the denominators of both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ vanish at $x=5,$ they are *not* analytic about $x=5.$ So, statement I is false.\n\n- The function\n\n$$\n(x-5)P(x)=(x-5)\\cdot \\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}-25}=\\frac{2x}{x+5}\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=5,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x-5)^{2}Q(x)=(x-5)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)}{{x}^{2}-25}=\\frac{(x-5)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+6)}{x+5}\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=5.$ Therefore, statement II is true.\n\n- Since statement I is false, $x=5$ is a singular point. Also, since statement II is true, it follows that $x=5$ is a **regular** singular point. Thus, statement III is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-342479/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e7d1368-06ee-5135-b9f4-d3d4d2756b32"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342479"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-342367/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "00aa35d1-5094-5d01-be03-24f6302453e3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342367/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" :answer/id #uuid "2bb492c7-72ff-5ccf-a349-c182870966a0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342367/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "93cc824e-ad5c-5aa8-8bb3-54f3f0aa6952" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342367/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "7ad6e103-713f-54a2-8fc3-b2334dbac23e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342367/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "24d8e7ec-f262-5663-9082-a272810fa9a3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342367/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5dcf657e-6d3d-5982-a00f-930819b55d19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-342367/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer-field/choices ["q-342367/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-342367/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" "q-342367/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-342367/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-342367/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342367" :question/id #uuid "5935deed-e890-5f0a-8031-fb6be5bc3579" :question/math-academy-id "q-342367" :question/problem "$$\n(x+2){y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x+2}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{3}}y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The functions $P(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{4}}$ are analytic about $x=-2.$\n- The functions $(x+2)P(x)$ and $(x+2)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=-2.$\n- The point $x=-2$ is an **irregular** singular point of the differential equation." :question/worked-solution "A singular point $a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x+2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2){y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x+2}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{3}}y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{4}}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{4}}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Since the denominators of both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ vanish at $x=-2,$ they are *not* analytic about $x=-2.$ So, statement I is false.\n\n- The function\n\n$$\n(x+2)P(x)=(x+2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{x+2}\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=-2$ since its denominator vanishes at $x=-2.$ Therefore, statement II is false.\n\n- Since statement I is false, $x=-2$ is a singular point. Also, since statement II is false, it follows that $x=-2$ is an **irregular** singular point. Thus, statement III is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-342367/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e7d1368-06ee-5135-b9f4-d3d4d2756b32"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342367"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-342300/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "8dcb794f-ed45-519e-81f1-cb46ff878f4c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342300/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" :answer/id #uuid "eb5a6a7d-8ed2-5030-aa03-7b4789e5918e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342300/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "a529a3b1-c5e2-54c1-9cd8-ff4497c00da9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342300/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "49b6e1e9-5404-53ca-8125-dc5e44416b63" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342300/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" :answer/id #uuid "c54e562f-81b5-592f-bae3-0ab3315aad35" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342300/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "12ed5850-768b-5945-b0f1-995b6a3f19f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-342300/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer-field/choices ["q-342300/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-342300/selection/431472f1dab61ef54de031c09a96fb6a21dc69c8bc09f12797d1d686e8c9e8e6" "q-342300/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-342300/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-342300/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342300" :question/id #uuid "c886c20d-db01-57b5-837f-db027fe4a87f" :question/math-academy-id "q-342300" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The functions $P(x)=\\frac{1}{x}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ are analytic about $x=0.$\n- The functions $xP(x)$ and ${x}^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=0.$\n- The point $x=0$ is an **irregular** singular point of the differential equation." :question/worked-solution "A singular point $x=a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by ${x}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Since the denominators of both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ vanish at $x=0,$ they are *not* analytic about $x=0.$ So, statement I is false.\n\n- The function\n\n$$\nxP(x)=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}=1\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=0,$ and the function\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}Q(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=0.$ Therefore, statement II is true.\n\n- Since statement I is false, $x=0$ is a singular point. Also, since statement II is true, it follows that $x=0$ is a **regular** singular point. Thus, statement III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-342300/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e7d1368-06ee-5135-b9f4-d3d4d2756b32"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342300"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-342299/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" :answer/id #uuid "0bb30b04-45c0-58ea-97aa-f653427c0d35" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342299/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "2d071e2a-a1ee-5fac-a816-15fc3fec85a2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342299/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "86174651-feb6-5cfc-bbf1-65fbc6fc84a7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342299/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "f3ec7b99-6a59-5c94-bc12-2134c17b070d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342299/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "36504f54-3878-50b5-925f-357951e1ba96" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342299/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1d0192d7-ba54-5eb9-b607-2896ece847ff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-342299/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer-field/choices ["q-342299/selection/93198c2e65472148f49c73a47d70892e6efd0b46acbb489ee2afeea42a4a8c25" "q-342299/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-342299/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" "q-342299/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" "q-342299/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342299" :question/id #uuid "4ad53237-ec99-5cc1-9169-b6a88cc54231" :question/math-academy-id "q-342299" :question/problem "$$\n({x}^{2}-1){y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The functions $P(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-1}$ and $Q(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}-1}$ are analytic about $x=0.$\n- The functions $xP(x)$ and ${x}^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=0.$\n- The point $x=0$ is a **regular** singular point of the differential equation." :question/worked-solution "A singular point $x=a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $({x}^{2}-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-1){y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-1}{y}^{′}+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}-1}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-1}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{2}-1}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=0.$ So, statement I is true.\n\n- Since $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=0,$ multiplying them by $x$ and ${x}^{2},$ respectively, they remain analytic about $x=0.$ Therefore, statement II is true.\n\n- Since statement I is true, $x=0$ is an ordinary point, not a singular point. Thus, statement III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-342299/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e7d1368-06ee-5135-b9f4-d3d4d2756b32"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342299"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-342644/field-1/711846b5f17da1e0f7b9c58c5ea67feac2751dddb68fa44a9c7038d9f8182937" :answer/id