3dfdf43848942eac6640bb67468ab45f1203ea562ecf7407161f894" "q-342435/field-3/711846b5f17da1e0f7b9c58c5ea67feac2751dddb68fa44a9c7038d9f8182937" "q-342435/field-3/dc7020e31a266715738fd921e33efbfc7b16ec3ae5c4b09a04e838c06763119c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342435/field-4/711846b5f17da1e0f7b9c58c5ea67feac2751dddb68fa44a9c7038d9f8182937" :answer/id #uuid "887fc55b-23f6-5bb6-9107-c65373cf55b4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "an ordinary" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342435/field-4/dc7020e31a266715738fd921e33efbfc7b16ec3ae5c4b09a04e838c06763119c" :answer/id #uuid "e39bae1e-7f6c-510b-8246-8dbffcf4c392" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "a regular singular" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342435/field-4/1cdd626053dfdf43848942eac6640bb67468ab45f1203ea562ecf7407161f894" :answer/id #uuid "b701af2e-247f-536e-9453-0ce5bb66fe03" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "an irregular singular" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342435/field-4" :answer-field/id #uuid "12a598f1-969c-575d-ba5d-b42e4f97297e" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-342435/field-4/711846b5f17da1e0f7b9c58c5ea67feac2751dddb68fa44a9c7038d9f8182937" :answer-field/choices ["q-342435/field-4/711846b5f17da1e0f7b9c58c5ea67feac2751dddb68fa44a9c7038d9f8182937" "q-342435/field-4/dc7020e31a266715738fd921e33efbfc7b16ec3ae5c4b09a04e838c06763119c" "q-342435/field-4/1cdd626053dfdf43848942eac6640bb67468ab45f1203ea562ecf7407161f894"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342435" :question/id #uuid "1caf1bbe-a462-5b0e-b414-1ffe7c759c9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-342435" :question/problem "$$\nx(x-1)(x+3){y}^{″}+\\frac{x(x-1)}{x+3}{y}^{′}+(x+3){e}^{x}\\,y=0\n$$\n\nClassify the following points for the differential equation given above.$\\,$The point $x=-3$ is\n{{field-1}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=0$ is\n{{field-2}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=1$ is\n{{field-3}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=3$ is\n{{field-4}}\npoint of the differential equation." :question/worked-solution "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nA singular point $x=a$ is regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $x(x-1)(x+3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(x-1)(x+3){y}^{″}+\\frac{x(x-1)}{x+3}{y}^{′}+(x+3){e}^{x}\\,y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{(x+3)^{2}}{y}^{′}+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x(x-1)}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{(x+3)^{2}}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x(x-1)}.\n$$\n\nThe function $P(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $(x+3)^{2}=0,$ which occurs at $x=-3.$\n\nThe function $Q(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x(x-1)=0,$ which occurs at $x=0$ and $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, $x=3$ is $\\text{an ordinary}$ point of the differential equation, and the singular points are\n\n$$\nx=-3,\\,x=0,\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's classify each singular point.\n\n- For $x=-3,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x+3)P(x)=(x+3)\\cdot \\frac{1}{(x+3)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{x+3},\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=-3.$ Thus, $x=-3$ is $\\text{an irregular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=0,$ the function\n\n$$\nxP(x)=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{(x+3)^{2}},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=0,$ and the function\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}Q(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{x}}{x(x-1)}=\\frac{x{e}^{x}}{x-1},\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=0.$ Therefore, $x=0$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=1,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x-1)P(x)=(x-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{(x+3)^{2}},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=1,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x-1)^{2}Q(x)=(x-1)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{x}}{x(x-1)}=\\frac{(x-1){e}^{x}}{x},\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=1.$ Therefore, $x=1$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-342435/field-1" "q-342435/field-2" "q-342435/field-3" "q-342435/field-4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "71f8b1c9-7856-52fa-8990-a83fda9ef2a9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342435"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-342421/field-1/711846b5f17da1e0f7b9c58c5ea67feac2751dddb68fa44a9c7038d9f8182937" :answer/id #uuid "1269ec15-69d2-50fc-85d8-5aafbd97fa40" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "an ordinary" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342421/field-1/dc7020e31a266715738fd921e33efbfc7b16ec3ae5c4b09a04e838c06763119c" :answer/id #uuid "25debacc-7ee2-54f2-9c23-779754963f07" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "a regular singular" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342421/field-1/1cdd626053dfdf43848942eac6640bb67468ab45f1203ea562ecf7407161f894" :answer/id #uuid "7cb8c503-e586-5373-bea6-a178ea77cc5f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "an irregular singular" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342421/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "d1fa6b54-160f-5ce5-a457-bbee3f5b4ba0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-342421/field-1/1cdd626053dfdf43848942eac6640bb67468ab45f1203ea562ecf7407161f894" :answer-field/choices ["q-342421/field-1/711846b5f17da1e0f7b9c58c5ea67feac2751dddb68fa44a9c7038d9f8182937" "q-342421/field-1/dc7020e31a266715738fd921e33efbfc7b16ec3ae5c4b09a04e838c06763119c" "q-342421/field-1/1cdd626053dfdf43848942eac6640bb67468ab45f1203ea562ecf7407161f894"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342421/field-2/711846b5f17da1e0f7b9c58c5ea67feac2751dddb68fa44a9c7038d9f8182937" :answer/id #uuid "960807fc-441a-5a57-846b-f4cd6fa512