d/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-342421/field-3/1cdd626053dfdf43848942eac6640bb67468ab45f1203ea562ecf7407161f894" :answer-field/choices ["q-342421/field-3/711846b5f17da1e0f7b9c58c5ea67feac2751dddb68fa44a9c7038d9f8182937" "q-342421/field-3/1cdd626053dfdf43848942eac6640bb67468ab45f1203ea562ecf7407161f894" "q-342421/field-3/dc7020e31a266715738fd921e33efbfc7b16ec3ae5c4b09a04e838c06763119c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342421/field-4/1cdd626053dfdf43848942eac6640bb67468ab45f1203ea562ecf7407161f894" :answer/id #uuid "5ce08b76-d7e6-5c52-bcbb-0e4c117afa48" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "an irregular singular" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342421/field-4/dc7020e31a266715738fd921e33efbfc7b16ec3ae5c4b09a04e838c06763119c" :answer/id #uuid "11348acf-2ccb-5e08-93be-a959cfbf93ee" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "a regular singular" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342421/field-4/711846b5f17da1e0f7b9c58c5ea67feac2751dddb68fa44a9c7038d9f8182937" :answer/id #uuid "e5c07e82-2b31-5506-b92b-201eea7e5791" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "an ordinary" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342421/field-4" :answer-field/id #uuid "cf56b2ce-08c5-5a28-940d-068c1c5c08de" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-342421/field-4/dc7020e31a266715738fd921e33efbfc7b16ec3ae5c4b09a04e838c06763119c" :answer-field/choices ["q-342421/field-4/1cdd626053dfdf43848942eac6640bb67468ab45f1203ea562ecf7407161f894" "q-342421/field-4/dc7020e31a266715738fd921e33efbfc7b16ec3ae5c4b09a04e838c06763119c" "q-342421/field-4/711846b5f17da1e0f7b9c58c5ea67feac2751dddb68fa44a9c7038d9f8182937"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342421" :question/id #uuid "cf12a8b3-3c24-5613-ab08-847e6fe908f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-342421" :question/problem "$$\n(x-1)(x+2)(x-4){y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{(x-1)(x+2)}{y}^{′}+(x-1)(x-4)y=0\n$$\n\nClassify the following points for the differential equation given above.$\\,$The point $x=-2$ is\n{{field-1}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=0$ is\n{{field-2}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=1$ is\n{{field-3}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=4$ is\n{{field-4}}\npoint of the differential equation." :question/worked-solution "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nA singular point $x=a$ is regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x-1)(x+2)(x-4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)(x+2)(x-4){y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{(x-1)(x+2)}{y}^{′}+(x-1)(x-4)y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}(x+2)^{2}(x-4)}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{x+2}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}(x+2)^{2}(x-4)}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{x+2}.\n$$\n\nThe function $P(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $(x-1)^{2}(x+2)^{2}(x-4)=0,$ which occurs at $x=-2,$ $x=1,$ and $x=4.$\n\nThe function $Q(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x+2=0,$ which occurs at $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, $x=0$ is $\\text{an ordinary}$ point of the differential equation, and the singular points are\n\n$$\nx=-2,\\,x=1,\\,x=4.\n$$\n\nNow, let's classify each singular point.\n\n- For $x=-2,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x+2)P(x)=(x+2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}(x+2)^{2}(x-4)}=\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}(x+2)(x-4)},\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=-2.$ Thus, $x=-2$ is $\\text{an irregular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=1,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x-1)P(x)=(x-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}(x+2)^{2}(x-4)}=\\frac{1}{(x-1)(x+2)^{2}(x-4)},\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=1.$ Therefore, $x=1$ is $\\text{an irregular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=4,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x-4)P(x)=(x-4)\\cdot \\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}(x+2)^{2}(x-4)}=\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}(x+2)^{2}},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=4,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x-4)^{2}Q(x)=(x-4)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+2}=\\frac{(x-4)^{2}}{x+2},\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=4.$ Therefore, $x=4$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-342421/field-1" "q-342421/field-2" "q-342421/field-3" "q-342421/field-4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "71f8b1c9-7856-52fa-8990-a83fda9ef2a9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342421"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-342438/field-1/711846b5f17da1e0f7b9c58c5ea67feac2751dddb68fa44a9c7038d9f8182937" :answer/id #uuid "40f032b7-7616-5823-853b-5249c0722039" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "an ordinary" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342438/field-1/1cdd626053dfdf43848942eac6640bb67468ab45f1203ea562ecf7407161f894" :answer/id #uuid "87f976e6-4525-5276-995d-045affafdac1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "an irregular singular" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342438/field-1/dc7020e31a266715738fd921e33efbfc7b16ec3ae5c4b09a04e838c06763119c" :answer/id #uuid "e772fd98-0e1e-567d-aeef-87667968c5e4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "a regular singular" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342438/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "414562e9-3a5c-5a04-89dc-93012901ad9b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-342438/field-1/1cdd626053dfdf43848942eac6640bb67468ab45f1203ea562ecf7407161f894" :answer-field/choices ["q-342438/field-1/711846b5f17da1e0f7b9c58c5ea67feac2751dddb68fa44a9c7038d9f8182937" "q-342438/field-1/1cdd626053dfdf43848942eac6640bb67468ab45f1203ea562ecf7407161f894" "q-342438/field-1/dc7