uuid "aa7f4c32-0d9f-5a04-b3b7-f45a458c7b18" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "an irregular singular" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342438/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "fdf6e8f0-25e6-5898-beaf-f8e591b45ee9" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-342438/field-3/1cdd626053dfdf43848942eac6640bb67468ab45f1203ea562ecf7407161f894" :answer-field/choices ["q-342438/field-3/711846b5f17da1e0f7b9c58c5ea67feac2751dddb68fa44a9c7038d9f8182937" "q-342438/field-3/dc7020e31a266715738fd921e33efbfc7b16ec3ae5c4b09a04e838c06763119c" "q-342438/field-3/1cdd626053dfdf43848942eac6640bb67468ab45f1203ea562ecf7407161f894"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342438/field-4/dc7020e31a266715738fd921e33efbfc7b16ec3ae5c4b09a04e838c06763119c" :answer/id #uuid "59edb6b7-3331-51ff-9aeb-48604d2a76f6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "a regular singular" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342438/field-4/1cdd626053dfdf43848942eac6640bb67468ab45f1203ea562ecf7407161f894" :answer/id #uuid "38e26114-6a23-55b9-9925-1c8c1c1c47fe" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "an irregular singular" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342438/field-4/711846b5f17da1e0f7b9c58c5ea67feac2751dddb68fa44a9c7038d9f8182937" :answer/id #uuid "77c7b723-cc0d-554f-ad17-492adf4b3cb9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "an ordinary" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342438/field-4" :answer-field/id #uuid "de2f0cd5-6f0b-571f-9dab-e00ce6add106" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-342438/field-4/711846b5f17da1e0f7b9c58c5ea67feac2751dddb68fa44a9c7038d9f8182937" :answer-field/choices ["q-342438/field-4/dc7020e31a266715738fd921e33efbfc7b16ec3ae5c4b09a04e838c06763119c" "q-342438/field-4/1cdd626053dfdf43848942eac6640bb67468ab45f1203ea562ecf7407161f894" "q-342438/field-4/711846b5f17da1e0f7b9c58c5ea67feac2751dddb68fa44a9c7038d9f8182937"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342438" :question/id #uuid "33389159-3f75-5b82-b87a-bf3acf44ba0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-342438" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{2}(x-7)^{2}(x+1){y}^{″}+(x+1){y}^{′}+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x(x-7)}y=0\n$$\n\nClassify the following points for the differential equation given above.$\\,$The point $x=7$ is\n{{field-1}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=-1$ is\n{{field-2}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=0$ is\n{{field-3}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=1$ is\n{{field-4}}\npoint of the differential equation." :question/worked-solution "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nA singular point $x=a$ is regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by ${x}^{2}(x-7)^{2}(x+1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}(x-7)^{2}(x+1){y}^{″}+(x+1){y}^{′}+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{x(x-7)}y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}(x-7)^{2}}{y}^{′}+\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{3}(x-7)^{3}(x+1)}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}(x-7)^{2}}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{3}(x-7)^{3}(x+1)}.\n$$\n\nThe function $P(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where ${x}^{2}(x-7)^{2}=0,$ which occurs at $x=0$ and $x=7.$\n\nThe function $Q(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where ${x}^{3}(x-7)^{3}(x+1)=0,$ which occurs at $x=-1,$ $x=0,$ and $x=7.$\n\nTherefore, $x=1$ is $\\text{an ordinary}$ point of the differential equation, and the singular points are\n\n$$\nx=-1,\\,x=0,\\,x=7.\n$$\n\nNow, let's classify each singular point.\n\n- For $x=-1,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x+1)P(x)=(x+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}(x-7)^{2}}=\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}(x-7)^{2}},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=-1,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x+1)^{2}Q(x)=(x+1)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{e}^{x}}{{x}^{3}(x-7)^{3}(x+1)}=\\frac{(x+1){e}^{x}}{{x}^{3}(x-7)^{3}},\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=-1.$ Thus, $x=-1$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=0,$ the function\n\n$$\nxP(x)=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}(x-7)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{x(x-7)^{2}},\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=0.$ So, $x=0$ is $\\text{an irregular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=7,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x-7)P(x)=(x-7)\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}(x-7)^{2}}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}(x-7)},\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=7.$ Therefore, $x=7$ is $\\text{an irregular singular}$ point of the differential equation." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-342438/field-1" "q-342438/field-2" "q-342438/field-3" "q-342438/field-4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "71f8b1c9-7856-52fa-8990-a83fda9ef2a9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342438"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-342643/field-1/711846b5f17da1e0f7b9c58c5ea67feac2751dddb68fa44a9c7038d9f8182937" :answer/id #uuid "f71f9a46-5e2b-5d41-82e5-00a662ebfa97" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "an ordinary" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342643/field-1/1cdd626053dfdf43848942eac6640bb67468ab45f1203ea562ecf7407161f894" :answer/id #uuid "a96aec39-a6fc-56ea-905d-f16762114f57" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "an irregular singular" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342643/field-1/dc7020e31a266715738fd921e33efbfc7b16ec3ae5c4b09a04e838c06763119c" :answer/id #uuid "f9f788ea-4c72-584c-bd44-a664d88b7202" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "a regular singular" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342643/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "693936ec-5be5-5ae2-bf41-449f97d45170" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-342643/field-1/711846b5f17da1e0f7b9c58c5ea67feac2751dddb68fa44a9c7038d9f8182937" :answer-field/choices ["q-342643/field-1/711846b5f17da1e0f7b9c58c5ea67feac2751dddb68fa44a9c7038d9f8182937" "q-342643/field-1/1cdd626053dfdf43848942eac6640bb67468ab45f1203ea562ecf7407161f894" "q-342643/field-1/dc7020e31a266715738fd921e33efbfc7b16ec3ae5c4b09a04e838c06763119c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342643/field-2/1cdd626053dfdf43848942eac6640bb67468ab45f1203ea562ecf7407161f894" :answer/id #uuid "89c6987d-7cac-5292-bd17-d8b4a411ae07" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "an irregular singular" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342643/field-2/711846b5f17da1e0f7b9c58c5ea67feac2751dddb68fa44a9c7038d9f8182937" :answer/id #uuid "805f81e6-5c29-5aef-aad8-75b5db8f4d9c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "an ordinary" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342643/field-2/dc7020e31a266715738fd921e33efbfc7b16ec3ae5c4b09a04e838c06763119c" :answer/id #uuid "7021c175-2247-58a4-b673-435a798dd75b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "a regular singular" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342643/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "3fe45fe1-b5d4-5fa1-a678-d54596aa8b2b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-342643/field-2/dc7020e31a266715738fd921e33efbfc7b16ec3ae5c4b09a04e838c06763119c" :answer-field/choices ["q-342643/field-2/1cdd626053dfdf43848942eac6640bb67468ab45f1203ea562ecf7407161f894" "q-342643/field-2/711846b5f17da1e0f7b9c58c5ea67feac2751dddb68fa44a9c7038d9f8182937" "q-342643/field-2/dc7020e31a266715738fd921e33efbfc7b16ec3ae5c4b09a04e838c06763119c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342643/field-3/dc7020e31a266715738fd921e33efbfc7b16ec3ae5c4b09a04e838c06763119c" :answer/id #uuid "1f8fb01f-352a-5f1a-9488-9fa20d0f857d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "a regular singular" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342643/field-3/1cdd626053dfdf43848942eac6640bb67468ab45f1203ea562ecf7407161f894" :answer/id #uuid "32437afc-1511-5ed2-b20e-3ae03fa4456c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "an irregular singular" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342643/field-3/711846b5f17da1e0f7b9c58c5ea67feac2751dddb68fa44a9c7038d9f8182937" :answer/id #uuid "504666d4-cfa2-5ced-b29a-9de735290ecd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "an ordinary" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342643/field-3" :answer-field/id #uuid "89680d7d-ad02-5a5d-9a85-48e9a8418015" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-342643/field-3/1cdd626053dfdf43848942eac6640bb67468ab45f1203ea562ecf7407161f894" :answer-field/choices ["q-342643/field-3/dc7020e31a266715738fd921e33efbfc7b16ec3ae5c4b09a04e838c06763119c" "q-342643/field-3/1cdd626053dfdf43848942eac6640bb67468ab45f1203ea562ecf7407161f894" "q-342643/field-3/711846b5f17da1e0f7b9c58c5ea67feac2751dddb68fa44a9c7038d9f8182937"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342643/field-4/dc7020e31a266715738fd921e33efbfc7b16ec3ae5c4b09a04e838c06763119c" :answer/id #uuid "c90ebe38-e5d9-518a-ad15-5a1901886921" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "a regular singular" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342643/field-4/1cdd626053dfdf43848942eac6640bb67468ab45f1203ea562ecf7407161f894" :answer/id #uuid "f2b9c2fe-0224-57f5-a892-ee2a5ed63ab9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "an irregular singular" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342643/field-4/711846b5f17da1e0f7b9c58c5ea67feac2751dddb68fa44a9c7038d9f8182937" :answer/id #uuid "cb1f396d-67c8-56a2-b07a-602f8ba64aec" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "an ordinary" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-342643/field-4" :answer-field/id #uuid "089440cb-4f93-51b4-b221-b3ea78625f65" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "q-342643/field-4/dc7020e31a266715738fd921e33efbfc7b16ec3ae5c4b09a04e838c06763119c" :answer-field/choices ["q-342643/field-4/dc7020e31a266715738fd921e33efbfc7b16ec3ae5c4b09a04e838c06763119c" "q-342643/field-4/1cdd626053dfdf43848942eac6640bb67468ab45f1203ea562ecf7407161f894" "q-342643/field-4/711846b5f17da1e0f7b9c58c5ea67feac2751dddb68fa44a9c7038d9f8182937"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-342643" :question/id #uuid "04c6ddbf-a561-50d8-a014-5a2210f9d08f" :question/math-academy-id "q-342643" :question/problem "$$\nx(x-6)(x+2){y}^{″}+x(x-6){y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}y=0\n$$\n\nClassify the following points for the differential equation given above.$\\,$The point $x=3$ is\n{{field-1}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=0$ is\n{{field-2}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=-2$ is\n{{field-3}}\npoint of the differential equation.\n\n$\\,$The point $x=6$ is\n{{field-4}}\npoint of the differential equation." :question/worked-solution "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+P(x){y}^{′}+Q(x)y=f(x)\n$$\n\nif both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nA singular point $x=a$ is regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^{2}Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $x(x-6)(x+2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(x-6)(x+2){y}^{″}+x(x-6){y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x+2}{y}^{′}+\\frac{1}{x(x-6)(x+2)^{3}}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\frac{1}{x+2}\\,\\text{and}\\,Q(x)=\\frac{1}{x(x-6)(x+2)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nThe function $P(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x+2=0,$ which occurs at $x=-2.$\n\nThe function $Q(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x(x-6)(x+2)^{3}=0,$ which occurs at $x=0,$ $x=6,$ and $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, $x=3$ is $\\text{an ordinary}$ point of the differential equation, and the singular points are\n\n$$\nx=0,\\,x=-2,\\,x=6.\n$$\n\nNow, let's classify each singular point.\n\n- For $x=0,$ the function\n\n$$\nxP(x)=x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+2},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=0,$ and the function\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}Q(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x-6)(x+2)^{3}}=\\frac{x}{(x-6)(x+2)^{3}},\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=0.$ Thus, $x=0$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=-2,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x+2)P(x)=(x+2)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+2}=1,\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=-2,$ but the function\n\n$$\n(x+2)^{2}Q(x)=(x+2)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x-6)(x+2)^{3}}=\\frac{1}{x(x-6)(x+2)},\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=-2.$ Therefore, $x=-2$ is $\\text{an irregular singular}$ point of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=6,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x-6)P(x)=(x-6)\\cdot \\frac{1}{x+2},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=6,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x-6)^{2}Q(x)=(x-6)^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{x(x-6)(x+2)^{3}}=\\frac{x-6}{x(x+2)^{3}},\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=6.$ Therefore, $x=6$ is $\\text{a regular singular}$ point of the differential equation." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-342643/field-1" "q-342643/field-2" "q-342643/field-3" "q-342643/field-4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "71f8b1c9-7856-52fa-8990-a83fda9ef2a9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-342643"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21847"] :question/problem "$$\n(x-2)(x+3)y'' + (x+1)y' + xy = 0\n$$\n\nWhich of the following is a **singular** point of the differential equation above?\n\n- $x=-3$\n\n- $x=0$\n\n- $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' + P(x)y' + Q(x)y = f(x)\n$$\n\nif both coefficients $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x-2)(x+3){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)(x+3){y}^{″}+(x+1){y}^{′}+xy & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{x+1}{(x-2)(x+3)}{y}^{′}+\\frac{x}{(x-2)(x+3)}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x) = \\dfrac{x+1}{(x-2)(x+3)}\n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\nQ(x) = \\dfrac{x}{(x-2)(x+3)}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check the analyticity of these functions at the given points.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=-3,$ since both denominators at $x=-3$ equal zero. So, $x=-3$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Both* functions are analytic about $x=0.$ So, $x=0$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation.\n\n- *Neither* function is analytic about $x=2,$ since both denominators at $x=2$ equal zero. So, $x=2$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21848"] :question/problem "$$\n(x-5)y'' + \\dfrac{x+5}{x-5}y' + \\dfrac{3}{(x-5)^3}y = 0\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- The functions $P(x)=\\dfrac{x+5}{(x-5)^2}$ and $Q(x)=\\dfrac{3}{(x-5)^4}$ are analytic about $x=5.$\n\n- The functions $(x-5)P(x)$ and $(x-5)^2Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=5.$\n\n- The point $x=5$ is an **irregular** singular point of the differential equation." :question/worked-solution "A singular point $a$ of the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' + P(x)y' + Q(x)y = f(x)\n$$\n\nis regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^2Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $(x-5){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-5){y}^{″}+\\frac{x+5}{x-5}{y}^{′}+\\frac{3}{(x-5)^{3}}y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{x+5}{(x-5)^{2}}{y}^{′}+\\frac{3}{(x-5)^{4}}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x) = \\dfrac{x+5}{(x-5)^2}\n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\nQ(x) = \\dfrac{3}{(x-5)^4}.\n$$\n\nLet's now check each statement in turn.\n\n- Since the denominators of both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ vanish at $x=5,$ they are *not* analytic about $x=5.$ So, statement I is false.\n\n- The function\n\n$$\n(x-5)P(x) = (x-5) \\cdot \\dfrac{x+5}{(x-5)^2} = \\dfrac{x+5}{x-5}\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=5$ since its denominator vanishes at $x=5.$ Therefore, statement II is false.\n\n- Since statement I is false, $x=5$ is a singular point. Also, since statement II is false, it follows that $x=5$ is an **irregular** singular point. Thus, statement III is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-21849"] :question/problem "$$\nx(x-2)(x+1)y'' + x(x-2)y' + \\dfrac{\\sin x}{(x-2)^2}\\, y = 0\n$$\n\nGiven the points $x = -1,$ $x = 0,$ $x = 1,$ and $x = 2,$ indicate whether they are ordinary or singular points of the differential equation above. If they are singular points, indicate whether they are regular or irregular." :question/worked-solution "The point $x=a$ is an ordinary point of the differential equation\n\n$$\ny'' + P(x)y' + Q(x)y = f(x)\n$$\n\nif both $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, $x=a$ is a singular point of the differential equation.\n\nA singular point $x=a$ is regular if both $(x-a)P(x)$ and $(x-a)^2Q(x)$ are analytic about $x=a.$ Otherwise, the singular point is irregular.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the differential equation in standard form by dividing by $x(x-2)(x+1){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(x-2)(x+1){y}^{″}+x(x-2){y}^{′}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{(x-2)^{2}}y & =0 \\\\ {y}^{″}+\\frac{1}{x+1}{y}^{′}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x(x+1)(x-2)^{3}}y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\nP(x)=\\dfrac{1}{x+1}\n\n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\n\nQ(x)=\\dfrac{\\sin x}{x(x+1)(x-2)^3}.\n$$\n\nThe function $P(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x+1 = 0,$ which occurs at $x = -1.$\n\nThe function $Q(x)$ is *not* analytic about points where $x(x+1)(x-2)^3 = 0,$ which occurs at $x=-1,$ $x=0,$ and $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, $x=1$ is a *ordinary point* of the differential equation, and the *singular points* are\n\n$$\nx=-1, \\quad x=0, \\quad x=2.\n$$\n\nNow, let's classify each singular point.\n\n- For $x=-1,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x+1)P(x) = (x+1) \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{x+1} = 1,\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=-1,$ and the function\n\n$$\n(x+1)^2 Q(x) = (x+1)^2 \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sin x}{x(x+1)(x-2)^3} = \\dfrac{(x+1)\\sin{x}}{x(x-2)^3},\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=-1.$ Thus, $x=-1$ is a *regular singular point* of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=0,$ the function\n\n$$\nxP(x) = x\\cdot\\dfrac{1}{x+1} = \\dfrac{x}{x+1},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=0,$ and the function\n\n$$\nx^2Q(x) = x^2\\cdot\\dfrac{\\sin x}{x(x+1)(x-2)^3} = \\dfrac{x\\sin x}{(x+1)(x-2)^3},\n$$\n\nis also analytic about $x=0.$ Thus, $x=0$ is a *regular singular point* of the differential equation.\n\n- For $x=2,$ the function\n\n$$\n(x-2)P(x) = (x-2)\\cdot\\dfrac{1}{x+1} = \\dfrac{x-2}{x+1},\n$$\n\nis analytic about $x=2,$ but the function\n\n$$\n(x-2)^2Q(x) = (x-2)^2\\cdot\\dfrac{\\sin x}{x(x+1)(x-2)^3} = \\dfrac{\\sin x}{x(x+1)(x-2)},\n$$\n\nis *not* analytic about $x=2.$ Therefore, $x=2$ is an *irregular singular point* of the differential equation." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
